swbrd on Debian
===============

Firewall
--------
Every swbrd package installs its own tables as a fragment in /etc/nftables.d/.
Each fragment uses the declare-delete-define idiom, so applying one replaces
exactly its own tables and passes every other table through untouched.

/etc/nftables.conf belongs to the nftables package and so cannot be shipped by
any swbrd package.  It has to include the fragment directory or the rules will
only survive until the next boot:

    #!/usr/sbin/nft -f
    flush ruleset
    include "/etc/nftables.d/*.nft"

`./scripts/dev.sh nft` in the source tree pushes exactly that file.  The
postinst warns when the include is missing.

Host prerequisites not owned by any package
-------------------------------------------
The address plan assumes the host's own /64 is routed here and split up by more
specific routes.  On the reference host that is /etc/systemd/network/eth0.network,
whose `[Route] Destination=<your /64>, Type=unreachable` is what keeps the /96s
in the daemon packages from being shadowed by the default route.  Without it the
packaged routes will not do what you expect.

The site's actual prefixes live in conffiles, not in the packaged units:

    /etc/default/swbrd-*                              daemon arguments
    /etc/systemd/network/70-*.network.d/10-site.conf  the matching routes

Those two always have to agree.
